 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Hybrid Primal-Dual Method
-A hybrid method combining primal (vertices on edges) and dual (vertices inside cells) approaches to create intersection-free surfaces in adaptive octrees [S1, unofficial]. It creates a fan of triangles from the minimum edge $e$, connecting vertex $v_e$ (on the edge) to vertices $v_f$ (on faces containing $e$) and dual vertices $v_c$ (inside cells) [S1, unofficial]. Fans of eight or six triangles are created for each minimum edge [S1, unofficial]. A key pitfall is the excessive number of polygons created per fan; Ju et al. [2006] provide geometric rules to substitute fans with fewer triangles within a valid envelope [S1, unofficial].
+A variant that combines primal (vertices on edges) and dual (vertices inside cells) approaches to create intersection-free surfaces in adaptive octrees [S1, unofficial]. It creates a fan of triangles from the minimum edge $e$, connecting vertex $v_e$ (on the edge) to vertices $v_f$ (on faces containing $e$) and dual vertices $v_c$ (inside cells) [S1, unofficial]. A key pitfall is that this method can create an excessive number of polygons per fan (typically eight or six triangles per minimum edge) [S1, unofficial]. To mitigate this, geometric rules from Ju et al. [2006] are used to substitute fans with fewer triangles within a valid envelope [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-- **Memory:** The method increases the number of vertices and faces in the resulting mesh [S1, unofficial]. For Level 6, vertices increase from 85,220 to 87,138 (+2.2%) and triangles from 41,690 to 44,596 (+7%) [S1, unofficial]. For Level 7, vertices increase from 299,016 to 302,839 (+1.3%) and triangles from 148,188 to 154,248 (+4.1%) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Time:** Processing time for meta-cells increases slightly [S1, unofficial]. For Level 6, meta-cell processing time increases from 144s to 154s (+10s) and the connection step increases by 2s [S1, unofficial]. For Level 7, processing time increases from 161s to 171s [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-- **Strengths:** Ensures a self-intersection-free surface, which is critical for volume rendering and simulation [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Increases mesh complexity (vertices and faces) compared to regular ambiguity treatments [S1, unofficial]. The regular ambiguity treatment does not increase vertex or face count but only ensures manifoldness [S1, unofficial].
-
-**When to Use:**
-- Use when constructing isosurfaces from adaptive octrees where self-intersections are unacceptable [S1, unofficial].
-- Use when the scene requires exact Euclidean distance calculations without propagation errors [S1, unofficial].
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-- Avoid if mesh size is a critical constraint and self-intersections can be tolerated or handled by other means [G].
-- Avoid if processing time is extremely limited, as the geometric checks add overhead [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds a small overhead to the meta-cell processing time. In tests on a salt dome volume, processing time increased from 144s to 154s (+10s) for Level 6 trees, and from 161s to 171s for Level 7 trees [S1, unofficial]. The connection step increased by only 2 seconds [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The method increases the number of vertices and faces in the resulting mesh. For Level 6, vertices increased from 85,220 to 87,138 (+2.2%) and triangles from 41,690 to 44,596 (+7%) [S1, unofficial]. For Level 7, vertices increased from 299,016 to 302,839 (+1.3%) and triangles from 148,188 to 154,248 (+4.1%) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Guarantees a self-intersection-free surface in adaptive constructions, which is critical for visual quality and further processing [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Increases mesh size compared to non-resolution methods. The hybrid variant can produce excessive polygons if geometric rules are not applied [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Use when generating isosurfaces from adaptive octrees where geometric validity is paramount and self-intersections are unacceptable. Not suitable when minimal mesh size is the primary constraint and self-intersections can be tolerated or removed by other means.
 
 ## Background
-The method is based on the work of Ju et al. [2006], who proposed a hybrid primal-dual approach for adaptive octrees [S1, unofficial]. It was implemented and evaluated in the context of extracting isosurfaces from salt domes [S1, unofficial]. The implementation uses a lookup table for Dual Marching Cubes configurations to identify ambiguous faces [S1, unofficial].
+The method is based on the work of Ju et al. [2006], who proposed a hybrid primal-dual approach for extracting intersection-free isosurfaces from adaptive octrees [S1, unofficial]. It was implemented and evaluated in the context of extracting salt dome isosurfaces from massive binary volumes, as described in [S1, unofficial]. The method extends the regular ambiguity treatment by addressing the specific challenges of adaptive grids, where trivial triangulations are insufficient to prevent self-intersections [S1, unofficial].
 
